    "guide.add-site.branding1": "A site on your internet is a folder. Give one a README, a manifest, a history or a note for the agents, and {wiki} writes it an article.",
    "guide.add-site.branding2": "Choose what the folder holds, and the figure answers with the indexer&apos;s own rules: its kind, whether it earns an article, the lead {wiki} would write, where its summary comes from, which rows the infobox shows, which sections appear, how it is filed and how search sees it.",
    "guide.add-site.branding3": "Five steps from left to right: a folder, a README, a manifest, a git history and pnpm index, ending in a new {wiki} article.",
    "guide.add-site.branding4": ", {wiki} tells them apart the way Wikipedia does.",
    "guide.add-site.branding5": "Every folder here has a README. {wiki} has nothing to ask for.",
    "guide.add-site.branding6": "You can help {wiki}. These are the largest pages still written from the folder alone. A README of twenty-five words gives each a summary and an Overview, turns a stub into an article",
    "guide.contribute.branding1": "{app} is small enough to hold in your head: one crawler, one file, one search engine and an encyclopedia drawn from it.",
    "guide.how-it-works.branding1": "Folders on disk flow into the indexer, which writes one file, data/index.json. Two readers, search and {wiki}, read that file and show it to you.",
    "guide.how-it-works.branding2": "; the server reads that one file twice, as search results and as {wiki} pages, and you read both.",
    "guide.how-it-works.branding3": "{app} is a personal internet. Its web is the folders on this machine; its search engine finds them the way you find pages, and its encyclopedia, {wiki}, gives every project an article and every other folder a stub. Nothing leaves the machine.",
    "guide.how-it-works.branding4": "{wiki}",
    "guide.how-it-works.branding5": "As an {wiki} article",
